WhatsApp is adding a way to hold a message and send it later. The company announced scheduled messages on 25 November 2026, calling it one of its most requested features. For everyday chats it is a small, useful change. For a shop or service that runs on WhatsApp, there is a reported catch to check first, covered below.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the cited pages for figures, dates and wording. This is not a full human review. WhatsApp is owned by Meta, which builds AI products that compete with Anthropic's. Stackbrook did not test any feature described here and has no affiliate relationship with any company mentioned at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated. This is general information, not legal or financial advice.*

## What WhatsApp announced

According to the WhatsApp Blog, you can set a message to go out anywhere from 10 minutes to 14 days after you write it. It works in one-to-one chats and in groups, and the announcement lists text, photos and videos. The person receiving it sees it like any other message.

WhatsApp also says three things that matter in daily use. A scheduled message is still delivered if your phone is switched off or out of battery. It stays end-to-end encrypted, and the company says that not even WhatsApp can read it. You can see your upcoming scheduled messages and delete them before they send.

The rollout is described as global and happening over the coming weeks. There is no single date, so it is normal if the option has not reached your phone. Digital Trends reports that it covers iPhone and Android.

## How to schedule a message

1. Open the chat or group and write your message, or add the photo or video.
2. Press and hold the send button.
3. Choose the date and time you want it to arrive.

To check what is waiting, Digital Trends, citing WhatsApp's Help Center, says to tap your profile picture and open Scheduled messages. Press and hold an item there to delete it. The same report notes that iPhone beta testers in October found the list inside the chat info screen, so the layout may differ.

The report also says you cannot edit a scheduled message at present. If the wording or time is wrong, delete it and write a new one. The WhatsApp Blog mentions viewing and deleting, but says nothing about editing either way.

If you write to people in other countries, check the date and time carefully when you pick them. The pages we read do not explain how the picker handles time zones, so we would not assume it converts to the recipient's local time. Digital Trends also reports that only people who were in the chat when you wrote the message will get it, so someone who joins later would miss it. WhatsApp's announcement does not say that, so treat it as unconfirmed.

## The WhatsApp Business catch

Digital Trends reports that, according to the Help Center, the WhatsApp Business app is left out for now. We could not open that scheduling help page ourselves, and the WhatsApp Blog announcement we read does not mention the Business app. Treat the exclusion as reported, check your own app, and do not plan holiday reminders around a button you cannot see. No date for adding it was given in anything we read.

Business users do have other tools. They solve different problems, so it helps to know which is which.

![Table comparing WhatsApp scheduled messages, Business app away messages and marketing messages by what starts them, who gets them and the main limit or cost](/images/site1/whatsapp-scheduled-messages-how-to-business-app-options-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the cited pages.*

## Away messages: automatic replies, not timed sends

An away message answers a customer who writes to you while you are busy or closed. It does not start a conversation at a time you choose. WhatsApp's help page gives these Android steps:

1. Open Tools and choose Away message.
2. Switch on Send away message.
3. Edit the wording by tapping the message, then confirm with OK.
4. Tap Schedule and choose Always send, Custom schedule, or Outside of business hours. The last choice needs business hours set in your business profile.
5. Tap Recipients and choose Everyone, Everyone not in address book, Everyone except, or Only send to.
6. Save your changes.

The phone or other device needs an active internet connection for away messages to go out. WhatsApp's holiday tips page also suggests putting your return date or expected reply time in the away message, adding a seasonal greeting message, and saving quick replies for common questions such as opening hours or payment methods.

## Marketing messages: paid and can be scheduled

If you want to send an offer to customers at a set time, WhatsApp's help page describes a marketing message tool that includes a Schedule button (the page refers to WhatsApp Business terms but does not name which app version has it). It may not be available in your area yet. Key points from that page:

- Each message needs at least a photo, and Meta reviews it before it is sent.
- You can pick contacts from smart lists, your address book, broadcast lists or labels.
- The limits are up to 500 contacts per message, 250 messages in the first 24 hours of using the feature, and 7,000 messages in any 30 days. WhatsApp says limits may vary by user.
- It is not free. You pay for messages that are delivered, but an amount covering every selected contact is reserved when you send or schedule, and funds for messages that cannot be delivered are credited back to your account for future use.
- Your device must be online at the scheduled time. If it is not, the app tries again after one minute, then every 10 minutes, for up to 10 tries in total.
- Processing after you send or schedule usually takes a few seconds but can take up to 3 hours.

That online requirement differs from personal scheduled messages, which WhatsApp says go out even with the phone off. The same page says businesses must give required notices, get consent where needed and honour opt-outs. Check the price shown in your app before you send or schedule, since the page we read does not list one.

## Which one should you use?

- **A birthday note or a reminder to a friend or group:** a scheduled message, once your app shows it.
- **Telling customers you are closed:** an away message.
- **A promotion to many customers at a chosen time:** the marketing message tool, if it is available to you and you have their consent.
- **A one-off customer reminder on the Business app:** send it by hand for now unless your app offers scheduling.

Before the December holidays, take two minutes to open a chat with yourself, type a test message, then press and hold the send button and see whether a scheduling option appears. If it does not, update WhatsApp from your app store and check again later. Business app users may still not see it, given the reported exclusion.
